package pane:extension@0.1.0;

// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
//
// The system's session and power commands (the Windows power features'
// System Commands, #125, ADR 0040): locking the screen, logging out,
// restarting, shutting down, sleeping, hibernating, turning the displays
// off and starting the screen saver, which any extension may call, as it
// calls the applications or the clipboard (ADR 0015), whatever its mode.
// Rust commands use them through pane-extension
// (`pane_extension::system_commands`), JavaScript and TypeScript ones
// through `@pane-app/extension/system-commands`
// (guests/js/system-commands.d.ts), imported only by a command whose
// bundle uses it. The specification names this interface
// `pane:extension/system`; that path is ADR 0037's system interface (the
// clipboard, opening, revealing and recycling, wit/system.wit), so this
// one is `system-commands`. The volume, microphone, Recycle Bin,
// appearance and device commands the specification also lists come as
// functions of their own here, which adds them without a breaking change.
//
// Each operation answers what it ended in: the state the system is in now
// (`done`), or why nothing changed (`explained`) — an operation that
// cannot happen on this system, or failed, is an answer, never an error
// and never a reason to pause the extension. The command tells the user
// with a HUD of that text (ADR 0035); the System Commands default
// extension confirms the destructive ones first (log out, restart, shut
// down), with "Don't ask again", as ADR 0040 records. Pane asks the
// system on a thread of its own, off the runtime's thread. The Windows
// adapter uses documented APIs only: `LockWorkStation`; `ExitWindowsEx`
// with a planned reason, the shutdown privilege enabled for the call
// alone, and applications forced closed for a restart and a shutdown (the
// user's choice, ADR 0040) but not for a log out; `SetSuspendState`, or
// the displays' monitor-power message on a computer that enters Modern
// Standby when they turn off; a monitor-power or screen-saver message sent
// with a timeout to a window of Pane's own, never a broadcast a hung
// application could block. The volume and microphone commands reach the
// audio endpoints through Core Audio (`IMMDeviceEnumerator`, and each
// endpoint's `IAudioEndpointVolume`). Other systems answer `explained`
// that the commands are not available there yet.
// The header follows `package`: comments above it would document the
// package, which extension.wit already does.

/// The session and power commands, as a command reaches them through Pane.
interface system-commands {
  /// What a command ended in. Its text is what the command shows the
  /// user.
  variant outcome {
    /// It happened: the state the system is in now, such as "Restarting".
    done(string),
    /// Nothing changed: why, such as that hibernation is not available on
    /// this computer, that no screen saver is set, or that the commands
    /// are not available on this system yet.
    explained(string),
  }

  /// Locks the screen: the user's session stays as it is, and signing in
  /// brings it back.
  lock-screen: func() -> outcome;

  /// Logs the user out. Applications are asked to close and given the
  /// chance to save, not forced: an application with unsaved work can
  /// hold the log out up (the user's choice, ADR 0040).
  log-out: func() -> outcome;

  /// Restarts the computer, forcing applications closed: an application
  /// with unsaved work cannot hold a restart up (the user's choice, ADR
  /// 0040).
  restart: func() -> outcome;

  /// Powers the computer off, forcing applications closed as a restart
  /// does (ADR 0040).
  shut-down: func() -> outcome;

  /// Sleeps the computer: on one that enters Modern Standby when its
  /// displays turn off, it turns them off and Windows enters its standby
  /// as it does by itself; otherwise it suspends the computer.
  sleep: func() -> outcome;

  /// Hibernates the computer, which a hibernation file makes possible;
  /// without one, nothing changes and the answer says so.
  hibernate: func() -> outcome;

  /// Turns the displays off. A monitor-power message to a window of
  /// Pane's own with a timeout, never a broadcast that a hung application
  /// could block. On a Modern Standby computer this is also how it sleeps.
  turn-off-displays: func() -> outcome;

  /// Starts the screen saver the user chose; when none is set, nothing
  /// changes and the answer says so.
  start-screen-saver: func() -> outcome;

  /// Raises the volume of the default output device by the step Windows'
  /// own volume keys take (2 of 100), never past 100. The answer says the
  /// volume it ended at, such as "Volume 52%".
  volume-up: func() -> outcome;

  /// Lowers the volume of the default output device by the same step,
  /// never below 0. The answer says the volume it ended at.
  volume-down: func() -> outcome;

  /// Mutes the default output device when it is not muted, unmutes it
  /// when it is. The answer says which it did: "Muted", or the volume it
  /// ended at, such as "Unmuted, Volume 52%".
  toggle-mute: func() -> outcome;

  /// Sets the volume of the default output device to `level`, 0 to 100;
  /// any other level changes nothing and the answer says why. The answer
  /// says the volume it ended at.
  set-volume: func(level: u8) -> outcome;

  /// Mutes every microphone when any of them is unmuted, and unmutes them
  /// all otherwise. The answer says which it did ("Microphones muted",
  /// "Microphones unmuted"); a microphone that has vanished since it was
  /// listed is skipped, and no microphone at all is explained.
  toggle-microphone-mute: func() -> outcome;

  /// Opens the Recycle Bin in File Explorer, every drive's together.
  /// The answer says the bin opened.
  open-recycle-bin: func() -> outcome;

  /// Empties the Recycle Bin, every drive's together, what it holds
  /// deleted for good. An already empty bin is a success, and the
  /// answer says so rather than saying anything failed.
  empty-recycle-bin: func() -> outcome;

  /// Toggles the system's appearance between light and dark: the
  /// personalization values for applications and for the system,
  /// together, followed by the setting-change broadcast, which is given
  /// a hang timeout so that a hung application cannot block it. The
  /// answer says which it chose: "Light mode", or "Dark mode".
  toggle-appearance: func() -> outcome;

  /// Toggles HDR: the displays' advanced colour state. If any
  /// HDR-capable display is off, they all turn on; if they are all on,
  /// they all turn off. The answer says the state it ended in ("HDR on",
  /// "HDR off"); no HDR-capable display at all is explained.
  toggle-hdr: func() -> outcome;

  /// Shows the desktop, as the shell's own toggle-desktop does: the
  /// windows open now hide, or come back if it is showing. The answer
  /// says the desktop was shown.
  show-desktop: func() -> outcome;

  /// Toggles whether File Explorer shows hidden files, refreshing its
  /// open windows. The answer says which it chose: "Hidden files shown",
  /// or "Hidden files hidden".
  toggle-hidden-files: func() -> outcome;

  /// Ejects every removable drive: each is locked, dismounted and
  /// ejected. A drive that fails is named with why, while the rest go
  /// ("Ejected E:; F: was not ejected: ..."); no removable drive at
  /// all is explained.
  eject-removable-drives: func() -> outcome;

  /// Toggles Bluetooth: if any radio is off they all turn on, and if
  /// they are all on they all turn off; a radio that has vanished since
  /// it was listed is skipped. The answer says the state it ended in
  /// ("Bluetooth on", "Bluetooth off"); no radio at all is explained.
  toggle-bluetooth: func() -> outcome;
}

/// The system-commands import alone, for guest bindings.
world system-commands-user {
  import system-commands;
}

/// The world Pane hosts: a command that may also use extension data, call
/// operations, find and open installed applications, run its native
/// helpers and system programs, list the folder granted to its package,
/// keep clipboard history, search the file index, run what the Run dialog
/// runs, and lock, log out, restart, shut down, sleep, hibernate or turn
/// off the displays of the computer (wit/system-commands.wit).
world extension-with-system-commands {
  include extension-with-run;
  import system-commands;
}
